NNAAMMEE
     ddeerrooffff — remove nroff/troff, eqn, pic and tbl constructs

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ddeerrooffff [--iikkppww] [--mm _a | _e | _l | _m | _s] [_f_i_l_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ddeerrooffff reads each file in sequence and removes all roff command lines,
     backslash constructions, macro definitions, eqn constructs (between “.EQ”
     and “.EN” lines or between delimiters), pic pictures, and table descrip‐
     tions and writes the remainder to the standard output.  ddeerrooffff follows
     chains of included files (“.so” and “.nx” commands); if a file has al‐
     ready been included, a “.so” is ignored and a “.nx” terminates execution.
     If no input file is given, ddeerrooffff reads from the standard input.

     The options are as follows:

     --ii      Ignore “.so” and “.nx” commands.

     --kk      Keep blocks of text intact.  This is the default behavior unless
             the --mm option is given.

     --mm      Enable support for common macro packages.  The --mm option takes
             the following arguments:

                   a       recognize man(7) macros.
                   e       recognize me macros.
                   l       remove list constructs.
                   m       recognize mm macros.
                   s       recognize ms macros.

     --pp      Preserve paragraph macros.  This option only has an effect if the
             --mm option is also specified.

     --ww      Output a word list, one ‘word’ (string of letters, digits, and
             apostrophes, beginning with a letter; apostrophes are removed)
             per line, and all other characters ignored.  Normally, the output
             follows the original, with the deletions mentioned above.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     man(7)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     ddeerrooffff first appeared outside of Bell Labs in PWB/UNIX 1.0.  It did not
     appear in freely redistributable BSD releases for licensing reasons.  Af‐
     ter Caldera relicensed early UNIX releases the 4.4BSD version was added
     to OpenBSD 3.1.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     Lorinda Cherry at the Bell Labs Computing Science Research Center.

BBUUGGSS
     ddeerrooffff is not a complete troff interpreter, so it can be confused by sub‐
     tle constructs.  Most errors result in too much rather than too little
     output.

     The --mmll option does not correctly handle nested lists.

